WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.760
Imagine turning a simple chat into a real desktop app you can actually install and use. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 238. Building a program from conversation memory comes next, then tools inside Slack, muse for shopping and Instagram messages, and quick bits. And Thropic has the decoder notes, turned Claude artifacts into programs that run on Mac and Windows computers. And Thropic is the company that makes Claude a chatbot you type with for homework.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.920
ideas or questions. Artifacts are the interactive projects Claude builds during a

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:40.300
chat, like games, calculators and study tools. Those programs can show a strip of

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.800
menus at the top, send alerts that pop up, and even open windows you can see

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.160
through. You build one in the chat without writing any code. The chat is the

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.380
notebook where you doodle an idea. The finished program is the framed picture on

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.920
your wall. Ready when the notebook is closed. Students can ask Claude for a

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.220
a history flashcard tool that pops up reminders at study time.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.700
Creative hobbies work too, like a drawing tool or a music mixer that lives on the computer

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.980
like any other program.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.480
A free account is enough to get started, and you do not need a paid plan.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:17.180
Open Claude in your browser and ask it to build a timer that tracks study sessions.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.440
Then look for the option to turn that artifact into an app.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.540
You can keep using it even when you are not chatting with Claude, at home or at school.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.266
Texting someone only leaves the words sitting on the screen.

00:01:29.806 --> 00:01:34.086
The chatbot holds every line of the whole conversation and uses that memory to build.

00:01:34.606 --> 00:01:39.206
It keeps a running sketch of what you want, and every extra detail you add makes that sketch

00:01:39.206 --> 00:01:39.686
sharper.

00:01:40.386 --> 00:01:44.786
Ask it to add a sound or make something bigger, and the sketch in its mind changes.

00:01:45.306 --> 00:01:47.006
Then you see the new version immediately.

00:01:47.646 --> 00:01:52.446
Over many back and forth messages, that sketch becomes a working little program with buttons

00:01:52.446 --> 00:01:53.106
you can click.

00:01:53.106 --> 00:01:55.346
It figures out the steps as it goes.

00:01:55.966 --> 00:02:00.026
From everything you have told it so far, the result can feel like something you built

00:02:00.026 --> 00:02:00.506
together.

00:02:01.026 --> 00:02:05.326
Your instructions have already become a complete set of rules the app follows on its own,

00:02:05.686 --> 00:02:08.426
so the program can keep working after you close the chat.

00:02:09.066 --> 00:02:13.446
Seeing it as a sketch that sharpens with every message makes the whole thing feel like teamwork

00:02:13.446 --> 00:02:14.626
you can actually follow.

00:02:15.575 --> 00:02:21.355
Victor is an AI described in a post on X that lives inside Slack and builds real software

00:02:21.355 --> 00:02:22.175
from your messages.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:26.235
Slack is a chat app teams use, like a group text with channels.

00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:30.535
You describe what you want, like turning a messy list into a working tracker.

00:02:31.015 --> 00:02:34.015
It handles the rest, including fixing problems on its own.

00:02:34.595 --> 00:02:39.215
People call this vibe coding, which means you describe the goal in chat and the AI writes

00:02:39.215 --> 00:02:40.235
the actual program.

00:02:40.775 --> 00:02:45.475
One user turned a simple spreadsheet into a full tool that now handles proposed changes,

00:02:46.115 --> 00:02:50.115
merges them, checks in on the work, and corrects issues without further help.

00:02:50.115 --> 00:02:54.435
Getting started with Victor is free, so you can experiment without paying.

00:02:55.055 --> 00:02:59.815
Open Slack, add the Victor app, and try typing a request to turn a copied Google sheet

00:02:59.815 --> 00:03:02.435
into a simple tool that tracks homework due dates.

00:03:03.115 --> 00:03:07.975
Watch what it creates and how it updates itself when you give feedback, all inside your regular

00:03:07.975 --> 00:03:10.895
Slack workspace with no extra website or download.

00:03:11.831 --> 00:03:17.051
Walmart teamed up with Meta, TechCrunch reports, so Muse can find deals without you switching

00:03:17.051 --> 00:03:17.371
apps.

00:03:17.371 --> 00:03:20.531
Meta is the company behind Instagram and Facebook.

00:03:21.211 --> 00:03:26.331
Muse is an AI agent, meaning a helper that can look at products, compare prices, and

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:27.711
suggest what fits your budget.

00:03:28.251 --> 00:03:32.431
If you already use Instagram or Facebook, you can reach Muse directly in those apps.

00:03:33.011 --> 00:03:35.091
Go to the Meta AI section in Instagram.

00:03:35.671 --> 00:03:40.451
Type Show Me Black Friday deals on headphones under $50 and see what it suggests.

00:03:41.191 --> 00:03:45.931
Muse stays in the same chat window you use for messaging, so shopping feels like a normal

00:03:45.931 --> 00:03:48.171
conversation rather than a separate task.

00:03:48.711 --> 00:03:53.051
On phones, jumping between store apps can feel slow during busy sale days.

00:03:54.094 --> 00:03:58.894
Direct messages on Instagram now carry mues, so you do not have to open a separate app.

00:03:59.274 --> 00:04:03.254
That makes it easy to ask quick questions or get help while you are already scrolling

00:04:03.254 --> 00:04:03.874
or messaging.

00:04:04.554 --> 00:04:09.834
Open Instagram, start a DM with yourself or someone, and ask mues to help you plan a study

00:04:09.834 --> 00:04:10.934
schedule for next week.

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:15.574
It can keep the conversation going right there in your messages, and you can switch back

00:04:15.574 --> 00:04:20.277
to talking with people without losing your place. Pinterest added a setting so

00:04:20.277 --> 00:04:25.017
parents can reduce how much AI-made content appears in a teen's feed. It is a

00:04:25.017 --> 00:04:28.957
direct response to worries about fake or unrealistic images showing up in

00:04:28.957 --> 00:04:33.437
searches and recommendations. If you use Pinterest, check the family settings for

00:04:33.437 --> 00:04:37.277
the new controls that let parents decide how much generated content reaches

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:42.277
younger accounts. That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert started

00:04:42.277 --> 00:04:43.857
it exactly where you are right now.

00:04:44.357 --> 00:04:47.537
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:04:47.877 --> 00:04:49.477
That's literally how learning works.

00:04:50.057 --> 00:04:53.017
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

